# gpu-lease TEST configuration: t3.micro, no GPUs, minutes instead of hours.
#
# This is for working through README -> "Verifying a deployment" without
# spending $0.53/hour/node on g4dn.xlarge. Everything about the control plane
# is exercised for real -- launch, readiness, peers over IMDS, the reaper, the
# budget, termination -- because none of that cares what the instance type is.
# What you cannot test here is anything that needs a GPU: nvidia-smi is absent
# on stock Ubuntu, so the boot script's GPU count comes back 0.
#
# Use it by name, which replaces gpulease.env rather than layering onto it.
# Create the database first -- setup.sh is what does that in production, and it
# only ever touches gpulease.env's:
#
#     export GPULEASE_ENV=gpulease.env.test
#     python -m gpulease.db init            # once, per GPULEASE_DB below
#     ./admin.py preflight                  # IAM: see the COURSE note
#     ./admin.py roster test-roster.csv     # mint yourself a token
#     .venv/bin/uvicorn gpulease.api:app --port 8001
#
# then point a CLI at it with GPULEASE_API=http://127.0.0.1:8001. Without the
# init step every admin read fails with `no such table: sessions`.
#
# Do NOT install this as gpulease.env on a host students are using -- the
# settings below are built for fast, cheap, destructive tests.

# --- identity -------------------------------------------------------------
# A SEPARATE COURSE TAG, and the most important line in this file. COURSE is
# the blast radius: every describe, stop and terminate this service performs is
# filtered by it. Sharing a tag with the live deployment would let a test run's
# `admin.py terminate` -- or a deliberately-passed deadline -- destroy real
# students' instances and disks.
#
# The cost of the separate tag is that the lease host's IAM role has to allow
# it. `StringEquals` takes a list, so install the policy with both:
#
#     sed 's/"REPLACE_COURSE_TAG"/["cs378","cs378-test"]/' iam-policy.json \
#       > /tmp/gpulease-policy.json
#
# Without that, RunInstances under this config is denied (an
# UnauthorizedOperation, surfaced by `./admin.py preflight`), which is the
# scoping working as designed rather than a bug.
GPULEASE_COURSE=cs378-test
# Must match the lease host's own region, exactly as in production: the host
# probes port 22 and authorises itself into the student security group by its
# own group id, which is a single-VPC reference.
GPULEASE_REGION=us-west-2

# --- serving --------------------------------------------------------------
# A different port and a different database, so this can run beside a live
# service without either one seeing the other's sessions.
GPULEASE_HOST=127.0.0.1
GPULEASE_PORT=8001
GPULEASE_DB=/tmp/gpulease-test.db

# --- what students get ----------------------------------------------------
# Blank = latest Ubuntu 22.04 LTS. No NVIDIA driver, which is fine here and
# useless on a real GPU type.
GPULEASE_AMI=

# ~$0.0104/hour in us-west-2. Two nodes for fifteen minutes is well under a
# cent, so the whole checklist costs less than the EBS it touches.
GPULEASE_INSTANCE_TYPE=t3.micro

# Stock Ubuntu 22.04's root snapshot is 8 GB, and VolumeSize is passed
# explicitly, so anything smaller fails every launch with
# InvalidBlockDeviceMapping. Raise this if you point GPULEASE_AMI at a bigger
# image.
GPULEASE_ROOT_GB=8

# t3.micro has no GPU. This is what the boot script exports as
# CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES and writes as `slots=` in the mpirun hostfile, so 1
# keeps those files shaped like the real thing to look at.
GPULEASE_GPUS_PER_NODE=1

# 2, deliberately: the multi-node cases (one subnet, peers over IMDS,
# cross-group isolation, partial cluster) are the ones most worth rehearsing,
# and they cost nothing at this instance type.
GPULEASE_NODES_PER_GROUP=2
GPULEASE_MASTER_PORT=29500

# Blank = every public subnet in the default VPC. Set these if the live
# deployment uses specific subnets and you want the test to launch beside it --
# they must auto-assign public IPs, or readiness never goes green.
GPULEASE_SUBNET_IDS=
GPULEASE_ALLOWED_SSH_CIDRS=0.0.0.0/0

# --- policy: minutes, not hours -------------------------------------------
GPULEASE_ACTIVE_ASSIGNMENT=test

# Blank on purpose. Checklist items 12-15 set this a minute or two ahead to
# test the deadline and termination; leaving a passed deadline in here would
# mean nothing starts and every run looks broken.
GPULEASE_DEADLINE=

# 0 = unlimited starts, so the hour budget is the ration and you can start and
# stop repeatedly without needing `admin.py grant` between attempts.
GPULEASE_MAX_STARTS=0

# 15 minutes. Long enough to ssh in and look around, short enough that a
# forgotten session cleans itself up while you are still at the keyboard.
GPULEASE_MAX_SESSION_HOURS=0.25

# 0.05 node-hours = 3 node-minutes = a 90-second lease at 2 nodes. This is
# checklist item 6: `start` and confirm the lease ends in ~90s rather than in
# MAX_SESSION_HOURS, which proves the budget cap is what bounds the session.
# Raise it to ~0.5 while testing anything else, or every session dies mid-test.
GPULEASE_GPU_HOUR_QUOTA=0.05

# 0 so the tiny budget above is actually usable. In production this is 15 and
# refuses a lease too short to be worth booting; here a too-short lease is the
# thing being tested.
GPULEASE_MIN_START_MINUTES=0

# --- termination -----------------------------------------------------------
# Off, matching the code default. Checklist item 13 confirms that a passed
# deadline only stops instances with this unset; item 14 turns it on with
# GPULEASE_TERMINATE_GRACE_HOURS=0 and confirms the volumes go. Turning it on
# here permanently would delete this config's instances every time you set a
# deadline, which makes the resume path untestable.
GPULEASE_TERMINATE_AT_DEADLINE=0
GPULEASE_TERMINATE_GRACE_HOURS=0

# --- reaper ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 20s rather than 120s. The reaper interval is the worst-case lag on every
# expiry, and at 120s each of the timing tests means two minutes of waiting.
GPULEASE_REAPER=1
GPULEASE_REAPER_INTERVAL=20
